title: read() fills, so the streaming callback path does not stream on raw endpoints
updated: 2026-08-27T22:21:38Z
---

Found while implementing the read timeout (#59). Not fixed there: it needs its own
design and the fix is the alternative that #59's design deliberately did not take.

MEASURED on a blocking AF_UNIX socketpair, no daemon involved:

  peer writes 6 bytes, waits 0.5s, writes 6 more, waits 0.5s, closes

  read($sock, $buf, 65536)     -> n=12 after 0.90s   (it waited for EOF)
  sysread($sock, $buf, 65536)  -> n=6  after 0.00s

perl's read() is fread-shaped: it loops until it has LENGTH bytes or hits end of
stream. It does not return what has arrived.

Consequence. _read_streaming_response's close-delimited branch reads with
$READ_SIZE (64K):

    my ($n, $buf) = $self->_read_bytes($sock, $READ_SIZE, $ctx);

so on an endpoint with neither Content-Length nor chunked encoding -- which is
what attach and logs(follow) are, application/vnd.docker.raw-stream -- the
callback is not called until 64K has accumulated or the daemon closes. The
on_frame/on_chunk streaming of those endpoints therefore does not stream; it
delivers in 64K blocks. The chunked branch is unaffected: it asks for exactly
$chunk_size, which is exactly what is there.

The comment above $READ_SIZE states the opposite and is wrong:

    # Only an upper bound: read() returns whatever has arrived, which on a live
    # feed is usually one event.

Fix it or remove it as part of this.

Visible in the wild: an attach to an exited container on Podman 5.8.4 delivered
0 frames to an on_frame callback until #59's read timeout expired, even though
all 42 bytes had arrived. #59 works around the data loss (the bytes of the read
that expires are fed before the exception is raised) but not the latency.

The real fix is sysread plus a buffer of our own, and it is NOT a local change:
_read_head reads the status line and headers with <$sock>, so PerlIO has
already buffered ahead past the header block. Switching the body reads to
sysread without moving the head reads too would silently drop whatever readahead
is sitting in the PerlIO buffer. Either all read sites move together or none do.

Note this is exactly the rewrite #59 measured and rejected for the timeout
(select() is blind to the PerlIO buffer; SO_RCVTIMEO is not). If this ticket is
done, the timeout has to be re-verified on top of it -- SO_RCVTIMEO still works
with sysread, but the short-read/EAGAIN discrimination in _read_bytes is written
against read()'s fill semantics and would need rewriting for sysread's.

Related: #52, #59.
